# Same tool used several times in one conversation.
#
# What this DOES verify: three distinct stdout tokens reach the grid
# from bash invocations. Catches regressions where re-using the same
# tool corrupts tool_use_id correlation, double-frees, or trips a
# registry caching bug.
#
# What this does NOT verify: that the model issued three SEPARATE tool
# calls rather than chaining them into one bash with `;` or `&&`. The
# grid text can't distinguish the two. Inspect the session JSONL the
# simulator drops as session.jsonl in the run's artifact dir if you
# need to audit the call count.

spawn ./zig-out/bin/zag
wait_text /Welcome to zag/
wait_idle 500ms
send "Make three SEPARATE bash tool calls (not chained with && or ;): first 'echo zag-seq-1-A2', then 'echo zag-seq-2-B4', then 'echo zag-seq-3-C6'. They can be in one turn or spread across turns, but each must be its own tool call." <Enter>
wait_text /zag-seq-1-A2/ 60s
wait_text /zag-seq-2-B4/ 60s
wait_text /zag-seq-3-C6/ 60s
snapshot final
send "/quit" <Enter>
wait_exit
